Root Cause

The vulnerability is caused by the combination of two insecure behaviors.

1. Unsafe prototype pollution

The application parses attacker-controlled URL parameters in a way that allows:

__proto__.property=value

to modify Object.prototype.

This allows the attacker to introduce arbitrary properties such as:

Object.prototype.sequence
2. Unsafe use of eval()

The application dynamically constructs JavaScript using:

eval()

and incorporates the attacker-influenced:

manager.sequence

into the generated code.

This turns prototype pollution into arbitrary JavaScript execution.

Impact

Successful exploitation allows an attacker to execute arbitrary JavaScript in the context of the vulnerable application.

Potential consequences include:

DOM XSS
Manipulation of the web page
Execution of actions in the victim's browser
Access to data available to JavaScript
Potential account compromise depending on the application's functionality

The actual impact depends on the privileges and sensitive functionality available within the affected origin.

Mitigation
Prevent prototype pollution

User-controlled parameters should not be allowed to modify object prototypes.

Properties such as:

__proto__
constructor
prototype

should be rejected or handled safely when processing untrusted input.

Avoid eval()

The application should avoid dynamically evaluating JavaScript:

eval(...)

Instead, explicit functions and validated data should be used.

Validate query parameters

Only expected query parameters should be processed.

Unexpected properties should be rejected rather than copied into application objects.

Use safer object structures

Where appropriate, objects that do not require prototype inheritance can be created using:

Object.create(null)

This reduces exposure to inherited properties from Object.prototype.

